# import-linter contracts.
#
# backend/ is the IMPORT ROOT, not a package: `core` and `finance` are top-level
# siblings (docs/ARCHITECTURE-PROPOSAL.md section D). That is why main.py does
# `from config import ...` and `from finance.api.routes import ROUTERS`, and why
# a relative import like `from ...core.money import Money` would resolve to a
# package that does not exist.
#
# Because there are two roots rather than one enclosing package, this file is
# the only place the module graph is described. Read it as the executable form of
# ARCHITECTURE.md section 3.
#
# Contract types available here: forbidden, layers, independence, protected,
# acyclic_siblings. There is no "private" type; a private module is expressed as
# a `protected` contract, which is what contract 1 does.
#
# Run with: uv run lint-imports

[importlinter]
root_packages =
    core
    finance

# The two alembic directories are not packages (no __init__.py) and hold no
# importable modules, so grimp never walks them. env.py is exec'd by Alembic with
# the .ini on sys.path, and its bare `context` references would fail if anything
# ever tried to import it.

# ═══ The layering, stated once ═════════════════════════════════════════
#
#     api  ->  ingestion  ->  domain  ->  core
#
# Arrows point inward. `core` depends on nothing above it. `domain` is pure and
# never reaches for the HTTP layer or the ingestion pipeline.

# ── Contract 0: core is the bottom of the graph ───────────────────────
#
# core holds shared primitives only (money, datetime, blob, preference). The
# moment core knows about a module's domain it stops being shareable, and every
# other module inherits the coupling.
#
# Stated as `forbidden` rather than `layers` because a layers contract needs a
# total ordering, and `api`/`ingestion`/`domain` are three independent
# sub-packages of one module — the layers contract below expresses that instead.
[importlinter:contract:0]
name = core does not depend on finance
type = forbidden
source_modules =
    core
forbidden_modules =
    finance
broken_contract_guidance = |
    core/ is the shared-primitive layer (money, datetime, blob, preference).
    Move the shared part down into core/ and the module-specific part up into
    finance/. Do not "fix" this by making finance a subpackage of core.

# ── Contract 1: finance.domain is PRIVATE ─────────────────────────────
#
# Nothing outside the finance module may import from finance.domain. The single
# export surface is finance.public, which carries protocols and read-only
# schemas only.
#
# Inside the module, api composes domain services and ingestion resolves records
# through them; that is the direction the layering above allows. finance.domain
# importing itself is excluded by the contract type (`as_packages` semantics),
# which is why internal cross-imports within domain do not appear as violations.
[importlinter:contract:1]
name = finance.domain is private to the finance module
type = protected
protected_modules =
    finance.domain
allowed_importers =
    finance
broken_contract_guidance = |
    finance.domain is PRIVATE. Other modules (health, tasks, a dashboard view)
    must depend on finance.public, which exports protocols and read-only schemas
    and nothing else. Add a protocol to finance/public.py and implement it in
    finance/, rather than importing finance.domain directly.

# ── Contract 2: dependencies point inward ─────────────────────────────
#
# api is the outermost layer. Nothing below it may import it: a domain service
# that reached for an HTTP route could not be tested without a request.
[importlinter:contract:2]
name = api is not imported by domain or ingestion
type = forbidden
source_modules =
    finance.domain
    finance.ingestion
forbidden_modules =
    finance.api
broken_contract_guidance = |
    The dependency arrows point inward. Move shared behaviour down into
    finance/domain or finance/ingestion and let the route call it, rather than
    having the lower layer call up into the router.

# ── Contract 3: ingestion is usable without the web layer ─────────────
#
# The concrete version of contract 2 for the pipeline. This is what lets the
# background worker run the import path with no FastAPI app in the process.
[importlinter:contract:3]
name = ingestion does not depend on api
type = forbidden
source_modules =
    finance.ingestion
forbidden_modules =
    finance.api
broken_contract_guidance = |
    The import pipeline must run in the background worker with no web framework
    loaded. If ingestion needs something from the API layer, that something is
    really domain logic — move it down.

# ── Contract 5: the public surface leaks nothing ──────────────────────
#
# finance.public exports protocols and read-only schemas. It must not pull in the
# implementation: an external module importing public.py should not transitively
# load SQLAlchemy, the adapters or FastAPI.
#
# This is the contract that keeps contract 1 honest. Without it, a convenient
# re-export in public.py would make the whole private layer public again.
[importlinter:contract:5]
name = public surface does not pull in the implementation
type = forbidden
source_modules =
    finance.public
forbidden_modules =
    finance.domain
    finance.ingestion
    finance.api
    finance.background
broken_contract_guidance = |
    finance/public.py exports protocols and read-only schemas. A Protocol is
    enough for a consumer to depend on; the implementation stays private. If you
    need a new type out here, define the shape here and implement it privately.

# ── Contract 6: the layering, as a total order ────────────────────────
#
# The same statement as contracts 0-3, expressed as one ordered list. Kept
# alongside them because it is the version that fails when someone inserts a new
# layer and forgets to say where it goes, and it reads top to bottom exactly as
# ARCHITECTURE.md section 3 does.
[importlinter:contract:6]
name = layering api -> ingestion -> domain -> core
type = layers
layers =
    finance.api
    finance.ingestion
    finance.domain
    core
broken_contract_guidance = |
    Each layer may import from itself and from the layers BELOW it in this list.
    finance.domain importing finance.ingestion means domain logic depends on the
    import pipeline; either the shared part belongs in core/, or the rule belongs
    in ingestion/ where the pipeline is.